1|0@relationships^1440206290#cubcj5g:damaskrose$:How much savings do you have? Check out r/financialindependence. If early retirement isn't an option, there are lots of others. Self-employment, freelancing, part-time work, working from home, alternating seasonal/contract work with long vacation periods, being a SAHP. Sounds like you're already on your way with your rental property and odd jobs. I recently quit my soul-sucking office job to start a micro-business out of my house. There are a lot of benefits to foregoing a typical 9-5 salary job, but the biggest downside is that it almost always comes with a much lower income. I'm assuming right now your parents are letting you live rent-free because of your injury, but would you be able to afford it if they started charging you rent? Are you willing to move to a smaller place if you can't? As long as you're realistic about having to live a more simple/minimalist lifestyle in order to match your lower earning potential, then I say go for it. For me, I ended up deciding no amount of creature comforts is worth the extra freedom of being self-employed. But unless you're already independently wealthy, you can't have it both ways. 
